\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability, yet understanding \emph{what} drives the value of routing decisions remains unclear. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to decompose routing signal into death-avoidance and non-death components. Across 245 ScienceWorld episodes (11 task types, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), death avoidance accounts for approximately 90\% of routing signal (DAF $= 90.41\%$ at $P{=}100$; Pearson $r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$), with 78.9\% of non-tie labels arising from death asymmetry alone. A trained router achieves only 57--59\% balanced accuracy, confirming thin learnable signal beyond survival. Cross-environment analysis on MATH ($N{=}300$) shows routing signal vanishes without a death penalty: the label distribution inverts from 86:9 (deterministic-preferred) to 30:17 (internal directionally favored), with a non-significant reversal of $+4.3$ percentage points. A Llama-3.1-8B pilot provides preliminary directional evidence that death-driven routing generalizes across model families. These findings indicate that in environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety---the router functions primarily as a safety critic rather than an efficiency optimizer.
\end{abstract}

\paragraph{Cross-environment scope.}
Our MATH and ALFWorld analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provide cross-environment evidence but do not conclusively establish generalization. In MATH (no death penalty), the deterministic advantage vanishes with a directional reversal favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$\,pp, not statistically significant). In ALFWorld, the deterministic advantage stems from a capability gap rather than death avoidance. Environments with graded failure costs or intermediate penalty regimes remain unstudied.

\paragraph{Step-level mislabeling.} Episode-level labels may assign suboptimal routing decisions to individual steps; in particular, steps preceding a death event are labeled det-preferred even if the death was not foreseeable from those steps' observations. Training on pure-strategy trajectories but deploying on mixed policy also introduces a distribution shift \citep{dagger-2011}.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Extending the counterfactual methodology to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents, or multi-turn dialogue systems would clarify how broadly death avoidance accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level labeling with denser rewards and the logit--generation gap in the implicit router are natural next steps. We audit labeling quality in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a publicly available text-based simulation environment, and does not involve human subjects or private data. All language models are accessed via standard APIs or publicly released weights. We report computational costs: approximately 40 GPU-hours on two RTX 5090 GPUs for data collection, router training, and evaluation.

